{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MR833K8uYtfX2p8WHfgkJk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# WI-286 — worker/integrator worktrees run the ambient interpreter, not the venv\n2\t\n3\t**WI:** WI-286 · **Status:** queued · **Delivers:** the unattended worktree\n4\tlaunch env (harness layer; sibling of WI-274) — no new spine SR\n5\t\n6\t## Problem\n7\t\n8\tThe dispatcher runs each train in its own git worktree\n9\t(`C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\<train>`) and each worker session shells out to\n10\trun the harness (pytest, `check.py`, the generators). The Python those commands\n11\tresolve is whatever is **ambient on the session's PATH** — nothing points a\n12\tworktree session at the repo's `./.venv`. In run `20260723T0202` every worker\n13\tinherited the launch shell's interpreter (**Python 3.8**, below the declared 3.11\n14\tfloor); it happened not to break because 3.8 passed most tests, but:\n15\t\n16\t- it is **below the WI-262/WI-270 floor** the repo declares and CI enforces, so a\n17\t  3.11-only idiom would pass locally in a worktree and only fail in CI;\n18\t- a worktree has **no `.venv`** of its own, so a bare `python -m pytest` there\n19\t  resolves to a system interpreter that may lack the pinned dev tools\n20\t  (`requirements-dev.txt`) — the pytest-cov/xdist the bar assumes;\n21\t- it compounds WI-285: once the integrator bar actually runs, it must run under a\n22\t  floor-satisfying, pinned interpreter or it re-imports this fragility.\n23\t\n24\tThis is the walk-away-run twin of WI-274 part A (which fixes the *primary*\n25\tcheckout's stale venv): the same \"which Python runs the harness\" question, at the\n26\tper-worktree boundary the dispatcher owns.\n27\t\n28\t## Approach\n29\t\n30\t- **Point worktree sessions at a floor-satisfying, pinned interpreter.** Options\n31\t  to weigh in the build (record the choice):\n32\t  - have the dispatcher pass the primary repo's `./.venv` interpreter into the\n33\t    worktree session env (e.g. prepend `<root>/.venv/Scripts` to PATH, or set the\n34\t    harness `{py}` to an absolute venv path) — the worktrees share the root venv,\n35\t    no per-worktree install;\n36\t  - or create/reuse a per-worktree venv (heavier; likely unnecessary since the\n37\t    kit is stdlib-only and only the *tests* need the pinned tools).\n38\t    Lean: share the root venv by absolute path — one pinned toolchain, zero\n39\t    per-train install cost.\n40\t- **Preflight the interpreter floor** at worker/integrator launch: refuse (or\n41\t  loudly warn + record) when the resolved harness Python is < 3.11, so a\n42\t  below-floor run never silently produces a green.\n43\t- Tests: a worktree session resolves the intended interpreter; a sub-3.11\n44\t  resolution is caught at preflight (mirrors WI-274's discovery tests).\n45\t\n46\t## Interfaces\n47\t\n48\tNo cross-module seam — intra-module (`agent_dispatch` worktree/session env +\n49\tpreflight, and tests). Sibling scope: WI-274 (dev-setup/venv) and WI-285\n50\t(integrator bar) share the \"which Python runs the harness\" concern.\n51\t\n52\t## Done-when\n53\t\n54\t- [ ] A worker/integrator worktree session resolves a ≥ 3.11, pinned-tool\n55\t      interpreter for the harness (verified: the run does not silently use\n56\t      ambient 3.8).\n57\t- [ ] A below-floor interpreter is caught at preflight, not after a false green.\n58\t- [ ] Decision recorded: shared root venv vs per-worktree; downstream/template\n59\t      disposition noted.\n60\t- [ ] Commit bar green; row `done`, spec archived.\n61\t\n62\t## Notes\n63\t\n64\t- Filed 2026-07-23 from the run-babysit. Consider building alongside WI-274\n65\t  (same env concern, different boundary) and before/with WI-285 (the integrator\n66\t  bar needs the right interpreter to be meaningful).\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90606481-3613-403f-a6ad-871cad87425e","uuid":"1e4e6d9d-bc4d-4b19-a6c8-e150be8a57e4","timestamp":"2026-07-24T10:54:24.538Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\p0-g3-WI-286-9253\\docs\\specs\\WI-286.md","content":"# WI-286 — worker/integrator worktrees run the ambient interpreter, not the venv\n\n**WI:** WI-286 · **Status:** queued · **Delivers:** the unattended worktree\nlaunch env (harness layer; sibling of WI-274) — no new spine SR\n\n## Problem\n\nThe dispatcher runs each train in its own git worktree\n(`C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\<train>`) and each worker session shells out to\nrun the harness (pytest, `check.py`, the generators). The Python those commands\nresolve is whatever is **ambient on the session's PATH** — nothing points a\nworktree session at the repo's `./.venv`. In run `20260723T0202` every worker\ninherited the launch shell's interpreter (**Python 3.8**, below the declared 3.11\nfloor); it happened not to break because 3.8 passed most tests, but:\n\n- it is **below the WI-262/WI-270 floor** the repo declares and CI enforces, so a\n  3.11-only idiom would pass locally in a worktree and only fail in CI;\n- a worktree has **no `.venv`** of its own, so a bare `python -m pytest` there\n  resolves to a system interpreter that may lack the pinned dev tools\n  (`requirements-dev.txt`) — the pytest-cov/xdist the bar assumes;\n- it compounds WI-285: once the integrator bar actually runs, it must run under a\n  floor-satisfying, pinned interpreter or it re-imports this fragility.\n\nThis is the walk-away-run twin of WI-274 part A (which fixes the *primary*\ncheckout's stale venv): the same \"which Python runs the harness\" question, at the\nper-worktree boundary the dispatcher owns.\n\n## Approach\n\n- **Point worktree sessions at a floor-satisfying, pinned interpreter.** Options\n  to weigh in the build (record the choice):\n  - have the dispatcher pass the primary repo's `./.venv` interpreter into the\n    worktree session env (e.g. prepend `<root>/.venv/Scripts` to PATH, or set the\n    harness `{py}` to an absolute venv path) — the worktrees share the root venv,\n    no per-worktree install;\n  - or create/reuse a per-worktree venv (heavier; likely unnecessary since the\n    kit is stdlib-only and only the *tests* need the pinned tools).\n    Lean: share the root venv by absolute path — one pinned toolchain, zero\n    per-train install cost.\n- **Preflight the interpreter floor** at worker/integrator launch: refuse (or\n  loudly warn + record) when the resolved harness Python is < 3.11, so a\n  below-floor run never silently produces a green.\n- Tests: a worktree session resolves the intended interpreter; a sub-3.11\n  resolution is caught at preflight (mirrors WI-274's discovery tests).\n\n## Interfaces\n\nNo cross-module seam — intra-module (`agent_dispatch` worktree/session env +\npreflight, and tests). Sibling scope: WI-274 (dev-setup/venv) and WI-285\n(integrator bar) share the \"which Python runs the harness\" concern.\n\n## Done-when\n\n- [ ] A worker/integrator worktree session resolves a ≥ 3.11, pinned-tool\n      interpreter for the harness (verified: the run does not silently use\n      ambient 3.8).\n- [ ] A below-floor interpreter is caught at preflight, not after a false green.\n- [ ] Decision recorded: shared root venv vs per-worktree; downstream/template\n      disposition noted.\n- [ ] Commit bar green; row `done`, spec archived.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Filed 2026-07-23 from the run-babysit. Consider building alongside WI-274\n  (same env concern, different boundary) and before/with WI-285 (the integrator\n  bar needs the right interpreter to be meaningful).\n","numLines":67,"startLine":1,"totalLines":67}}}
